  "tour": {
    "title": "Genesis 1: From Formless Darkness to God’s Image",
    "intro": "This tour builds a commentary on Genesis 1 by following the chapter’s movement: God forms and fills the world, gives humans a distinctive vocation, and brings creation to its seventh-day completion. It also lets you hear two interpretive lenses—the literary shape of the chapter and a Christian reading of its opening mention of God’s Word and Spirit—without confusing those lenses with the chapter’s central claims.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:4115d249-com-d21c89a6-dc42-301f-a389-af61511332ea",
        "startSec": 0,
        "endSec": 360,
        "why": "Begin with the chapter’s big questions and its literary architecture. The commentator says Genesis 1 asks “where did the world and everything in it come from?” and “what does it mean to be human?”, then explains that the first three days form the world and the next three fill it: “days one and four go together,” as do two and five, and three and six.",
        "episodeTitle": "Genesis 1:1-2:3",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/whbgtb54jm47282d/genesis_11-238ad37.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1416,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:4115d249-com-d21c89a6-dc42-301f-a389-af61511332ea",
        "startSec": 700,
        "endSec": 880,
        "why": "Now move from cosmic structure to humanity’s place within it. Reading Genesis 1:26–31, the speaker stresses the repeated phrase “in the image of God,” explicitly including “male and female,” and explains the image as God’s “representative presence” in his kingdom and temple. This gives the commentary its account of human dignity and vocation.",
        "episodeTitle": "Genesis 1:1-2:3",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/whbgtb54jm47282d/genesis_11-238ad37.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1416,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:aec71fd9715129af804d8566bea8a37a",
        "startSec": 150,
        "endSec": 330,
        "why": "Hear a distinct Christian interpretation of Genesis 1:1–2. The teacher notices “the presence of the Word or the Logos” alongside the Father and the Spirit moving over the waters, and argues that this is not God becoming a Trinity over time but “who he has always been.” Place this here as a theological reading that connects Genesis 1 to the wider Christian canon.",
        "episodeTitle": "40 Minutes In The Old Testament: Episode 2",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_Min_Ep_2.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2404,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:cathedral-time",
        "startSec": 2650,
        "endSec": 2830,
        "why": "Finish by widening the seventh day into a biblical-theological conclusion. The speakers connect Genesis 1’s seven divine speeches and Sabbath with the tabernacle’s seven commands, saying that on the seventh day God’s rest is also his presence “settling in.” This lands Genesis 1 not merely as a sequence of origins, but as an ordered world moving toward divine presence and rest.",
        "episodeTitle": "The Cathedral in Time",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/021dbf00-c41b-49cd-af81-b39dd2792a02/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=021dbf00-c41b-49cd-af81-b39dd2792a02",
        "durationSec": 3428,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips give you a commentary shaped by the text’s own progression: God answers formlessness and emptiness by forming and filling, places humans in the world as his image-bearing representatives, and brings the work to completion in Sabbath rest. The tour also leaves room to distinguish Genesis 1’s literary presentation from later theological reflection on the Father, Son, and Spirit.",
    "totalSeconds": 900
